1
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:06,170
 That's life.

2
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:27,070
 Approximately 5,000 million years ago,

3
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:30,740
 single-celled, the cell developed.

4
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,520
 Specimens though we are,

5
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:07,580
 we nonetheless retain many vestiges of our rapid development.

6
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:16,320
 For example, the liquid in which our cells bathe

7
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:20,400
 is almost identical to the seawater from which we originally come.

8
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:28,730
 Fingers used to be fish fins.

9
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:38,290
 Six is what remains of our monkey's tail.

10
00:03:38,290 --> 00:03:47,800
 Take the brain with its three layers.

11
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:50,970
 The first layer we inherited from the reptiles.

12
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:54,960
 The second, the Paleocortex, from the mammals.

13
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:00,140
 And the third, the Neocortex, the most important, developed very rapidly.

14
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:02,980
 It only took 500,000 years to form.

15
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,820
 But of course, you already know all about that.

16
00:04:06,210 --> 00:04:08,350
 So if you'd like to, we'll move backwards.

17
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:13,030
 Hey, where are you going? Stop at once. I said quads.

18
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:18,610
 A little faster, please.

19
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:32,670
 Right, we'll start again.

20
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:34,870
 Here it is, then.

21
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:38,370
 Primal soup, the source of all life.

22
00:04:38,370 --> 00:05:19,680
 The cell's engineer.

23
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:23,910
 I'll put one here.

24
00:05:26,530 --> 00:05:28,410
 They're here.

25
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:34,970
 genes are going to become chromosomes.

26
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:39,000
 Oh dear, that's not very strong.

27
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,860
 Oh, I have an idea.

28
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:46,050
 Let's see how this will work.

29
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:59,390
 Isn't that clever?

30
00:05:59,390 --> 00:06:05,980
 Come on, everybody!

31
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:13,100
 Will it hold or won't it?

32
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:14,460
 Yes, it will.

33
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:16,990
 Won't it?

34
00:06:16,990 --> 00:06:26,360
 Genes have become chromosomes.

35
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:37,150
 They will become the nucleus of the cell.

36
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:47,570
 Mitochondria, which are probably ancient bacteria,

37
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:50,270
 are surrounded by the cell which is forming.

38
00:06:50,270 --> 00:07:04,700
 A cell is born,

39
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:07,580
 but it has to multiply if life is to develop.

40
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,770
 Every living thing is composed of cells,

41
00:07:11,770 --> 00:07:15,250
 And the human body has 60,000,000 million of them.

42
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:21,550
 The skin, the bones, the nerves and the muscles, the heart and the brain are all composed of cells.

43
00:07:22,090 --> 00:07:29,170
 Each one an independent living organism, breathes, produces energy, feeds itself and reproduces itself.

44
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,340
 Size, one hundredth of a millimeter.

45
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:39,230
 For a cell to be visible to the naked eye, the human body would have to be 500 meters tall.

46
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:45,280
 And even then, it would be only the size of a pinhead.

47
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:50,810
 This is tissue formed of cells.

48
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,640
 Some salt. Does anyone need salt?

49
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:58,720
 Minerals. Get your minerals here.

50
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,740
 Glucocides over here. Come and get your glucocides.

51
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:02,560
 Would you like some?

52
00:08:03,570 --> 00:08:04,530
 Yeah, lovely.

53
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:13,090
 I'll take you to the cells.

54
00:08:32,070 --> 00:09:01,460
 Get away from here. We don't want any dust.

55
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:16,860
 Carry on.

56
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:18,970
 We need sugar immediately.

57
00:09:19,790 --> 00:09:22,740
 Add some fat, some proteins, and I think some water.

58
00:09:22,910 --> 00:09:24,480
 You heard what they did.

59
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:58,950
 Traffic school paid proper attention to everything we were taught.

60
00:10:05,490 --> 00:10:06,970
 Don't rush work.

61
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:11,540
 Come off, Fred.

62
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:13,970
 Anything to report, Lieutenant?

63
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:15,060
 Nothing, sir.

64
00:10:16,790 --> 00:10:18,500
 Keep well, Captain. Goodbye.

65
00:10:20,580 --> 00:10:24,190
 Here come the red blood corpuscles to bring us some oxygen.

66
00:10:25,330 --> 00:10:26,550
 Good, we need some.

67
00:10:29,210 --> 00:10:32,300
 Prefer doing that to carrying away the carbon dioxide.

68
00:10:45,190 --> 00:10:55,390
 Sigh there and see how a cell is made.

69
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:56,730
 But how, Globin?

70
00:10:56,870 --> 00:10:59,330
 There's no window and we're too big to get in there.

71
00:10:59,670 --> 00:11:02,700
 But we red globules are cells as well, you know.

72
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:10,240
 Lost our nucleus so we can't reproduce our cells like the others.

73
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:13,460
 It's another load of carbon dioxide.

74
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:44,930
 Standard.

75
00:11:44,930 --> 00:11:55,270
 They're now in the membrane that protects our cell, ladies and gentlemen.

76
00:11:55,570 --> 00:11:59,710
 And as you can probably see, it is composed mainly of proteins, fat, and sugar,

77
00:12:30,790 --> 00:12:52,940
 which are our protein assembly lines.

78
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:54,640
 We have thousands and thousands of those.

79
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:00,640
 Mitochondria, energy-producing centers.

80
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:04,370
 They produce it by burning up all the food that is brought here.

81
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:07,500
 And what is more, all our respiration comes from here too.

82
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:18,490
 The centriole, the command post that controls the unity or the division of the cell.

83
00:13:21,990 --> 00:13:31,790
 This ribbon is the RNA messenger.

84
00:13:31,790 --> 00:13:35,790
 You see, it carries all the orders from the control center to the assembly line.

85
00:13:36,110 --> 00:13:38,950
 And the control center, it just so happens, is right there.

86
00:13:43,970 --> 00:13:46,720
 Our wonderful storage depots.

87
00:13:47,380 --> 00:13:49,670
 You know, we produce enough for export as well.

88
00:13:56,970 --> 00:13:59,130
 Destination at last, one of our lysosomes.

89
00:13:59,610 --> 00:14:01,090
 This is where you will be transformed.

90
00:14:01,950 --> 00:14:03,170
 A new life awaits you.

91
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:08,480
 It's very sweet, too.

92
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,420
 Soon, you'll be just like them.

93
00:14:11,730 --> 00:14:12,210
 Idiot!

94
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,240
 What are you doing out of the way?

95
00:14:17,730 --> 00:15:07,850
 Service.

96
00:15:08,290 --> 00:15:09,450
 Will someone please tell me?

97
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:12,700
 All we can do, Chief, we've tried everything, believe me.

98
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:14,610
 Why don't you speed things up at the exits?

99
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:16,230
 You can see they all jammed.

100
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:19,510
 The assembly lines are saturated, Chief. They've asked us to slow down deliveries.

101
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:25,180
 Things can't carry on like this. Send all the soplers to the fats immediately.

102
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:26,600
 Come on, get rid of it.

103
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,260
 Will someone please connect me to the maestro as soon as possible?

104
00:15:38,610 --> 00:15:46,240
 I have to speak to the maestro immediately.

105
00:15:47,090 --> 00:15:49,150
 The maestro is very busy right now.

106
00:15:52,490 --> 00:15:56,740
 I have to speak to him.

107
00:15:58,650 --> 00:16:02,300
 I have to speak to him.

108
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:06,600
 What's happening? What's going on?

109
00:16:10,230 --> 00:16:12,270
 What is it? I'm listening.

110
00:16:13,110 --> 00:16:16,940
 The maestro, he says, look, they're swamped with food.

111
00:16:16,940 --> 00:16:19,030
 They can only stock fats.

112
00:16:20,350 --> 00:16:21,630
 Something's got to be done.

113
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:23,400
 And the other factories?

114
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:26,390
 I'm afraid the situation is the same everywhere, maestro.

115
00:16:31,540 --> 00:16:35,850
 To do what's necessary.

116
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:38,730
 Tell me, supervisor, are you asleep?

117
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:40,620
 You're letting everybody come in.

118
00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:42,520
 Don't you know our factories are overflowing?

119
00:16:42,910 --> 00:16:44,240
 Of course she doesn't know.

120
00:16:44,530 --> 00:16:45,710
 It's up to me to know.

121
00:16:45,950 --> 00:16:47,110
 I'm the boss, after all.

122
00:16:47,990 --> 00:16:48,790
 Listen to me.

123
00:16:48,950 --> 00:16:51,030
 From this moment on, you're to stop all imports.

124
00:16:51,170 --> 00:16:52,590
 Nothing more comes in, do you hear?

125
00:16:52,690 --> 00:16:53,330
 Yes, sir.

126
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:56,720
 Send me a neuron messenger.

127
00:16:58,400 --> 00:16:59,640
 Transmit this to the brain.

128
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:02,930
 If they want to keep making fat up there, it's their business.

129
00:17:03,170 --> 00:17:05,470
 After all, the responsibility is theirs, you know.

130
00:17:05,690 --> 00:17:08,770
 But I don't want them to come and complain afterwards that they were not warned.

131
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:17,790
 Anymore.

132
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:23,170
 Say, please let me in.

133
00:17:23,630 --> 00:17:25,550
 I've been told they need me inside there today.

134
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:59,550
 Through to sector 4,000, I want to speak to Lieutenant Jumbo Jr.

135
00:18:00,490 --> 00:18:03,370
 I've already spotted them, Captain.

136
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:04,730
 Don't worry, no problem.

137
00:18:44,120 --> 00:19:45,150
 How everything's coming along today, shall we?

138
00:19:45,150 --> 00:19:57,580
 We need some sugar, yes, and some oxygen, of course, some water, some amino acids, all right, some proteins.

139
00:19:57,740 --> 00:20:00,660
 Lots of those, naturally, they make up half our body substance.

140
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:04,630
 and I wonder why there's so much fat, that's odd.

141
00:20:04,630 --> 00:20:07,050
 Ah, a pinch of salt, that's good.

142
00:20:07,050 --> 00:20:08,810
 Well, all we have to do now is to hand over

143
00:20:08,810 --> 00:20:10,630
 to the membrane manager and remind him

144
00:20:10,630 --> 00:20:11,730
 that he must not forget--

145
00:20:11,730 --> 00:20:15,300
 - Sector 4000 calling all cells of Sector 4000.

146
00:20:15,300 --> 00:20:17,060
 Program the manufacture of collagen

147
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:20,560
 and send it out as soon as it's ready, over and out.

148
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:24,060
 - Hey, you there at headquarters, can't that order wait?

149
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:27,540
 I'd far rather see to my own needs first, hmm?

150
00:20:27,540 --> 00:20:29,730
 - This is an entire system.

151
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:31,570
 The skin has just been injured.

152
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:33,890
 You know that orders for exports have priority.

153
00:20:34,170 --> 00:20:36,120
 Well, you heard what he said, didn't you?

154
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:37,720
 Cancel everything and start again.

155
00:20:37,720 --> 00:20:40,880
 Chromosome room, an urgent order for collagen.

156
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:45,030
 Send RNA messengers with the production blueprints to the production centers.

157
00:20:45,030 --> 00:20:46,850
 Got that? Then jump to it.

158
00:20:46,850 --> 00:20:57,480
 I'm in here, racking you lazy chromosomes!

159
00:20:57,480 --> 00:21:12,460
 Get to work at once!

160
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:45,200
 What is it?

161
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:47,520
 What's happening? Wait!

162
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:50,430
 Oh, you must stop!

163
00:21:50,430 --> 00:21:57,430
 But once, you idiots,

164
00:21:57,430 --> 00:22:04,910
 said send the RNA messengers and you send the original DNA,

165
00:22:04,910 --> 00:22:06,670
 the base of our genetic capital.

166
00:22:06,670 --> 00:22:09,690
 Without that, how do you think we're going to reproduce ourselves?

167
00:22:09,950 --> 00:22:10,450
 Idiots!

168
00:22:10,690 --> 00:22:11,750
 Well, you heard what he said.

169
00:22:11,950 --> 00:22:13,150
 Will someone please...

170
00:22:13,150 --> 00:22:15,160
 You could have been so stupid!

171
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:20,750
 Perishable, but that genetic capital is what links us with eternity.

172
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:23,750
 Now, just make me a duplicate, a messenger.

173
00:22:24,070 --> 00:22:26,310
 And no mistakes this time, you imbeciles!

174
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:47,570
 Here.

175
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:49,890
 There you are.

176
00:22:59,990 --> 00:23:08,330
 ...in one of our cells.

177
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:12,080
 In one of our 60,000,000,000 cells.

178
00:23:12,220 --> 00:23:14,560
 Ready for dispatch. Send it.

179
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:16,390
 The cell.

180
00:23:17,370 --> 00:23:18,490
 Its size...

181
00:23:18,490 --> 00:23:19,890
 One hundredth of a millimeter.

182
00:23:20,820 --> 00:23:24,670
 every day 500,000 million of them vanish

183
00:23:24,670 --> 00:23:29,210
 and are replaced at the rate of 50 million each second.

